  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **quadratic formula** solves any quadratic equation, once it is written as $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$. Identify $a$, $b$ and $c$, substitute into $x=\\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}$, and simplify. The answer is left in **exact surd form** unless the expression under the root is a perfect square, when the solutions are rational.",
    "facts": [
      "Rearrange to $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$ first, then read off $a$, $b$ and $c$ — including their signs: $2y^{2}-9y=-3$ becomes $2y^{2}-9y+3=0$, so $c=3$.",
      "Substitute carefully with brackets: for $x^{2}-x-2=0$, $x=\\dfrac{-(-1)\\pm\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-2)}}{2(1)}$.",
      "The quantity $b^{2}-4ac$ is the **discriminant**. When it is a perfect square the roots are rational; otherwise they are surds; when it is negative there are no real solutions.",
      "Simplify the surd and cancel common factors: $\\dfrac{6\\pm\\sqrt{48}}{6}=\\dfrac{6\\pm4\\sqrt{3}}{6}=\\dfrac{3\\pm2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$.",
      "The whole numerator is divided by $2a$, not just the $-b$ term — keep the fraction bar under both parts."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rearrange the equation into the form $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$.",
      "Identify $a$, $b$ and $c$.",
      "Substitute into the quadratic formula and simplify."
    ]
  },
